stand user perceptions and opportune moments for more\u0002than-one-minute microlearning using proactive smart speakers at\nL34@P0: home. We first developed a proactive microlearning service through\nL35@P0: six pilot studies (n=29), and then conducted a three-week field study\nL36@P0: (n=28). We identified the key contextual factors relevant to oppor\u0002tune moments for microlearning of various durations, and discussed\nL37@P0: the design implications for proactive conversational microlearning\nL38@P0: services at home.\nL39@P0: CCS CONCEPTS\nL40@P0: • Human-centered computing → User interface management\nL41@P0: systems; Ubiquitous and mobile computing.\nL42@P0: KEYWORDS\nL43@P0: Smart Speakers, Microlearning, Conversational Interaction, Oppor\u0002tune Moment, Interruptibility\nL44@P0: ACM Reference Format:\nL45@P0: Minyeong Kim, Jiwook Lee, Youngji Koh, Chanhee Lee, Uichin Lee, and Auk\nL46@P0: Kim. 2024. Interrupting for Microlearning: Understanding Perceptions and\nL47@P0: ∗Equal contribution.\nL48@P0: †Corresponding authors.\nL49@P0: This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution International\nL50@P0: 4.0 License.\nL51@P0: CHI ’24, May 11–16, 2024, Honolulu, HI, USA\nL52@P0: © 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\nL53@P0: ACM ISBN 979-8-4007-0330-0/24/05\nL54@P0: https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642778\nL55@P0: Interruptibility of Proactive Conversational Microlearning Services. In Pro\u0002ceedings of the CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems\nL56@P0: (CHI ’24), May 11–16, 2024, Honolulu, HI, USA. ACM, New York, NY, USA,\nL57@P0: 21 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642778\nL58@P0: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL59@P0: Language learning requires substantial investment in time and ef\u0002fort [54]. Such demand poses challenges for individuals who strug\u0002gle to spend extra time on learning, often leading to difficulties\nL60@P0: in maintaining consistent and sustainable learning progress [18].\nL61@P0: Thereby, microlearning is a promising solution [9, 19, 52]. While for\nL62@P0: successful microlearning, learners need to participate frequently\nL63@P0: and repetitively [18, 59], the popularity of smart speakers in homes\nL64@P0: provides a new opportunity to seamlessly integrate bite-sized learn\u0002ing tasks into daily routines by proactively providing them with\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGetting Things Done: A Simple Step-By-Step Guide (https://www.todoist.com/productivity-methods/getting-things-done)\nciteturn33view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.todoist.com/productivity-methods/getting-things-done\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 546\nL0:   * 【0†Personal】\nL1:   * 【1†Business】\nL2:   * Integrations & AI\nL3: \nL4:     * 【2†AI tools】\nL5:     * 【3†AI agents】\nL6:     * 【4†Integrations】\nL7:     * 【5†API†developer.todoist.com】\nL8:   * Resources\nL9: \nL10:     * 【6†Download apps】\nL11:     * 【7†Templates】\nL12:     * 【8†Teams Toolkit】\nL13:     * 【9†Customer stories】\nL14:     * 【10†Help center】\nL15:     * 【11†Productivity methods + quiz】\nL16:     * 【12†Inspiration Hub】\nL17:   * 【13†Pricing】\nL18: \nL19:   * 【14†Log in】\nL20:   * 【15†Start for free】\nL21: \nL22:   * 【15†Start for free】\nL23:   *   * 【0†Personal】\nL24:   * 【1†Business】\nL25:   * Integrations & AI\nL26:     * 【2†AI tools】\nL27:     * 【3†AI agents】\nL28:     * 【4†Integrations】\nL29:     * 【5†API†developer.todoist.com】\nL30: \nL31:   * Resources\nL32: \nL33:     * 【6†Download apps】\nL34:     * 【7†Templates】\nL35:     * 【8†Teams Toolkit】\nL36:     * 【9†Customer stories】\nL37:     * 【10†Help center】\nL38:     * 【11†Productivity methods + quiz】\nL39:     * 【12†Inspiration Hub】\nL40: \nL41:   * 【13†Pricing】\nL42: \nL43: 【14†Log in】【15†Start for free】\nL44: # Getting Things Done (GTD)\nL45: \nL46: ## Systematize the clutter in your brain and get things done\nL47: \nL48: Jump to section\nL49: ## More resources\nL50: \nL51:   * 【16†Copy the GTD setup】\nL52:   * 【17†How a General Contractor Uses Todoist + GTD to Run His Small Business】\nL53: \nL54: 【18†Image: Illustration by Margarida Mouta†res.cloudinary.com】 Illustration by Margarida Mouta\nL55: \nL56: Todoist makes the world’s #1 task manager to organize your work and life. 【19†Tell me more】\nL57: \nL58: * * *\nL59: Do you ever feel like you spend more time thinking about doing your tasks than, well…actually doing them? Worrying about everything on your to-do list can be exhausting, especially when the list doesn’t seem to get any smaller. When information piles up in your head, you feel stressed, overwhelmed, and uncertain\nL60: Getting Things Done — or GTD for short — is a popular task management system created by productivity consultant David Allen based on a simple truth: the more information you have bouncing around inside your head, the harder it is to decide what to do first.\nL61: Allen observed that our brains are much better at processing information than storing it (\"your head's a crappy office\"). The GTD method lays out a workflow where you can dump all this mental clutter into an external system and organize it so you can confidently answer “What should I be working on?” at any given moment without worrying you might forget something important you need to do later.\nL62: ### Copy the GTD setup\nL63: \nL64: Todoist makes is easy to capture and organize all your \"open loops\". Copy our templated GTD setup in a click.\nL65: \nL66: 【16†See the setup】\nL67: \nL68: 【20†Image†images.ctfassets.net】\nL69: \nL70: Try GTD if you...\nL71: \nL72:   * Worry about forgetting small details\nL73: \nL74:   * Wear lots of hats in your job and life\nL75: \nL76:   * Get stressed by an unorganized workload\nL77: \nL78:   * Start lots of projects but have trouble finishing them\nL79: \nL80:   * Feel overwhelmed by the amount of things you need to keep track of\nL81:   * Seek a sense of control over personal and professional commitments\nL82: \nL83:   * Have never GTD'd before (everyone should GTD at least once in their lives 😉)\nL84: \nL85: In this guide, you’ll learn the art of Getting Things Done from principles to workflows along with the most intuitive way to implement them. We’ll focus on how to GTD with Todoist, a powerful duo if you’re ready to simplify your life, but the same principles apply no matter what app you use.\nL86: Prefer watching over reading? We've got you covered with a companion 【21†video for Getting Things Done†www.youtube.com】. Check out the video below. Or, if you enjoy the quiet solitude of reading, keep going for a deeper dive.\nL87: \nL88: 【22†Image†res.cloudinary.com】\nL89: \nL90: Play video\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiny Decisions - Apps on Google Play (https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.nixwang.tinydecisions)\nciteturn33view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.nixwang.tinydecisions\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 271\nL0: 【0†Games】【1†Apps】【2†Movies & TV】【3†Books】【4†Kids】\nL1: \nL2: 【0†google_logo Play】\nL3: \nL4: search\nL5: \nL6: help_outline\nL7: \nL8: 【5†Image†fonts.gstatic.com】\nL9: \nL10: 【0†Games】【1†Apps】【2†Movies & TV】【3†Books】【4†Kids】\nL11: \nL12: 【6†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】【6†Image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL13: \nL14: 【6†Image: Icon image†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL15: # Tiny Decisions\nL16: \nL17: 【7†TS Tech】\nL18: \nL19: Contains ads In-app purchases\nL20: \nL21: 3.4 star\nL22: \nL23: 4.73K reviews\nL24: \nL25: 【8†Image: Content rating†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL26: \nL27: Everyone\nL28: \nL29: info\nL30: \nL31: 1M+\nL32: \nL33: Downloads\nL34: \nL35: Install\nL36: \nL37: Share\nL38: \nL39: Add to wishlist\nL40: \nL41: 【9†Image: Content rating†play-lh.googleusercontent.com】\nL42: \nL43: Everyone\nL44: \nL45: 【10†Learn mor